@@ -0,0 +1,29 @@
+---
+id: dl-003-applypatch-applies-operations-to-a-deep-copy-and-returns-a-new-document
+type: decision-log
+title: "applyPatch applies operations to a deep copy and returns a new document"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Our services patch JSON documents with standard patches (RFC 6902). The input document is often shared
+with other code that still reads it, so `applyPatch` must not mutate it. RFC 6902 also applies the
+operations of a patch in order, each to the result of the previous one, and a patch fails as a whole
+when one operation fails.
+
+## Decision
+
+- `applyPatch(document, patch)` makes one deep copy of `document` up front, applies every operation to
+  that copy in order, and returns the copy.
+- The copy is discarded when an operation throws, so the caller's document is never partly patched.
+- Values taken from the patch (the `value` of `add`, `replace` and `test`) are copied into the result,
+  so later operations cannot change the caller's patch object either.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The input document and the patch are never mutated, and the caller can rely on that.
+- Each call copies the whole document. This costs time and memory on large documents; a caller that
+  patches a very large document in a hot loop would need an in-place variant, which can be added later
+  without changing this function.
