@@ -0,0 +1,33 @@
+---
+id: dl-004-applypatch-throws-an-error-for-an-invalid-patch-and-for-a-failed-operation
+type: decision-log
+title: "applyPatch throws an Error for an invalid patch and for a failed operation"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+## Context
+
+`applyPatch` must say when a patch is invalid, and when one of its operations cannot be applied. As in
+dl-002 for `resolvePointer`, a silent `undefined` or a partly patched document would be ambiguous and
+easy to ship by mistake.
+
+## Decision
+
+- `applyPatch` throws an `Error` in two cases, and its message names the operation index (for example
+  `patch[2]`) where one applies:
+  - the patch is invalid: it is not an array; an element is not an object; `op` is missing or is not
+    one of `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`, `test`; `path` is not a string; `value` is
+    missing for `add`, `replace` or `test`; `from` is not a string for `move` or `copy`; or a pointer
+    is not a valid JSON Pointer;
+  - an operation fails: its target does not exist (for `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`), an array
+    index is out of range, `test` finds a different value, `move` moves a location into its own
+    descendant, or `remove` is applied to the whole document.
+- Error messages reuse the wording of the `resolvePointer` errors (dl-002) where they overlap.
+- Errors are plain `Error` instances, as in dl-002.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Callers must handle a thrown error. There is no partial result to inspect.
+- Invalid patches and failed operations are not told apart programmatically, as with `resolvePointer`.
+  A subclass can be added later without breaking callers that catch `Error`.
