@@ -0,0 +1,37 @@
+---
+id: dl-005-applypatch-follows-rfc-6902-strictly-including-the-append-index-move-and-test-rules
+type: decision-log
+title: "applyPatch follows RFC 6902 strictly, including the append index, move and test rules"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+## Context
+
+The library is imported by other team projects, so its behaviour must follow the published standard it
+implements (README). RFC 6902 defines several rules that a looser implementation would get wrong.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Pointers are parsed with the same rules as `resolvePointer` (dl-001), so `applyPatch` accepts exactly
+  the pointers that `resolvePointer` accepts.
+- `add` into an array inserts before the index; the index may equal the array length, and `-` appends.
+  `add` into an object sets the member, replacing an existing one.
+- `add`, `replace` and `move` with the path `""` replace the whole document.
+- `remove` and `replace` require the target to exist; `remove` of the whole document is an error.
+- `move` requires `from` to exist, and rejects a `from` that is a proper prefix of `path` (a location
+  cannot be moved into its own child). Moving a location to itself leaves the document unchanged.
+- `copy` requires `from` to exist and copies its value.
+- `test` compares JSON values by structure: primitives by value, arrays element by element in order, and
+  objects by their members regardless of key order. Numbers are compared with `===`, so `0` and `-0`
+  are equal.
+- Members are created as own properties with `Object.defineProperty`, so a member named `__proto__` is
+  stored as data and does not change the prototype of the object.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Other projects get the standard's behaviour and can rely on it.
+- Pointers that a looser library would accept, such as `-` in `remove`, are rejected, as dl-001 already
+  does for `resolvePointer`. Callers that need leniency must normalise their patches first.
+- The `test` rule for numbers means `-0` and `0` are the same value. This follows JavaScript equality and
+  is not a separate JSON rule.
